      "keyInsight": "Most people think lifting a heavy object off the floor requires brute arm strength. That is completely backward. Your arms are not cranes. Your arms are merely steel cables attached to hooks. The real engine lives in your hips, glutes, and hamstrings. When you round your lower back, you turn your spine into an unstable fishing rod bent under pressure. Keep your spine rigid like an iron crowbar. The barbell must travel in a perfectly straight vertical line directly over the middle of your foot. If the bar swings forward even two inches, gravity suddenly gains immense leverage against you. It is like holding a heavy cast iron skillet by the very tip of its handle instead of close to the pan. Pull the bar right up against your shins. Keep the path vertical, lock your back tight, and push the floor away.",
      "content": "Imagine you are trying to lift a heavy wooden crate off your garage floor. If you stand two feet away and bend over, you will instantly strain your back. But if you stand right over the crate, drop your hips, and hold it close, it suddenly feels half as heavy. That is the deadlift in a nutshell. It is the most primal test of human strength on the planet. Gravity pulls straight down, so your job is to lift straight up along the shortest path possible.\n\nThink of your spine as an architectural crane. The crane tower does not bend or wiggle while hauling steel beams. It stays rock-solid so the pulleys and engines can do the actual lifting. When you set up for a deadlift, you squeeze your chest up to lock your entire spine into one solid block. Your arms hang straight down like tough steel chains. They do not bend. They simply transmit power from your legs and hips directly into the barbell.\n\nHere is the golden rule: the barbell must stay glued to the middle of your foot. That is your body's natural center of balance. If the bar drifts forward, you create an accidental lever arm that multiplies the load against your back. Drag the bar up your shins, slide it past your knees, and stand tall. You do not yank the weight with sudden panic. You smoothly push the floor away with your feet, and the world moves down as the bar moves up.",
