      "id": "starting-strength-basic-barbell-training-ch-6",
      "order": 6,
      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "The Power Clean: Snapping Speed Into Heavy Iron",
      "keyInsight": "Strength without speed is like a high-horsepower tractor that can only crawl at two miles per hour. The power clean is where raw strength transforms into explosive athletic horsepower. The paradox of the clean is that you cannot muscle the bar up with your arms. Your biceps are completely useless against a heavy load. Instead, the lift is an explosive vertical jump while holding weight in straight arms. You smoothly pull the bar off the floor past your knees. Then, the moment it touches mid-thigh, your hips and knees violently snap open like a spring-loaded mouse trap. That sudden burst of hip extension launches the barbell upward into weightless flight. All you do next is snap your elbows forward, catch the bar across your front shoulders, and absorb the impact with soft knees.",
      "content": "Imagine snapping a heavy bath towel to make it crack like a whip. If you pull your arm slowly, the towel merely flutters. But if you accelerate smoothly and snap your wrist at the final millisecond, you release an explosion of kinetic energy. The power clean works on that exact principle of instant acceleration. You do not rush the bar off the floor; you build speed progressively until the final explosive snap.\n\nThe first phase of the lift looks identical to a deadlift. The bar rises steadily from the floor to the middle of your thighs. During this first stage, your arms remain completely straight like hanging ropes. If you bend your elbows early, you bleed off all your leg power, just like trying to jump on a soft, squishy mattress.\n\nOnce the bar reaches the jump position at your mid-thigh, you explode upward with full fury. Your hips, knees, and ankles extend simultaneously in a fraction of a second. This violent jump propels the bar upward through momentum alone. In the blink of an eye, you whip your elbows around the bar and catch it cleanly across your front shoulder muscles. It teaches your brain and nervous system to recruit every muscle fiber instantly.",
